== Geography ==

Migjorn occupies the southern end of the Balboa isthmus, extending from the [[Balboa Canal]] corridor in the north to the southern coast at the Aethiopian Ocean. The province's topography is dominated by a broad coastal plain along the Aethiopian coast, which widens southward and is backed by rolling interior hills that rise toward the central highlands of the isthmus. The coastal plain is largely deforested, converted to agricultural use over centuries of plantation cultivation, though remnant mangrove forests survive along the estuarine sections of the coast.

The northern comarcas, along the canal corridor and the Estret border, contain the densest settlement and the principal transport infrastructure. The [[Balboa Canal]] enters the province from the northwest, near the comarca of [[L'Espigó (comarca)|L'Espigó]], and descends through a series of locks to the Aethiopian terminus at [[Sant Llàtzer]] in [[El Delta (comarca)|El Delta]]. The canal corridor is the most economically productive and densely settled part of the province.

The central comarcas are characterised by rolling agricultural land, with sugar cane and banana plantations dominating the landscape. The interior hills, rising to approximately 500–800 metres, support cacao and coffee cultivation in the cooler elevations, though much of the original forest cover has been removed for agricultural expansion.

The southern comarcas are the most remote and sparsely populated, with rugged terrain along the Sinchew border and the southern coast. The [[La Garrotxa (comarca)|La Garrotxa]] comarca in the southwest contains volcanic formations and rugged terrain that have limited agricultural development, while the [[Cap del Sud (comarca)|Cap del Sud]] comarca at the southern tip of the isthmus is the most isolated, with a long coastline and very low population density.

=== Península del Verger ===

{{main|Península del Verger}}

The northeastern portion of the province is occupied by the '''Península del Verger''', a flat, blocky limestone peninsula approximately 103 km east–west by 113 km north–south, covering roughly 7,880 km² across the comarcas of [[El Verger (comarca)|El Verger]], [[La Punta (comarca)|La Punta]], and [[El Cap (comarca)|El Cap]]. The peninsula is an uplifted {{w|coral reef|coral limestone}} platform — a former {{w|Miocene}}-era reef system raised above sea level by tectonic compression during the formation of the Isthmus of Balboa, when the colliding continental plates closed the ancient seaway. The naturally flat reef surface was preserved as the platform was lifted en masse, and the blocky outline results from the harder limestone resisting coastal {{w|erosion}} while softer surrounding marine sediments were worn away by wave action over millions of years.

The {{w|karst}} geology of the peninsula means that rainwater drains underground through fissures and caves rather than forming surface rivers, producing the characteristically dry, flat terrain. The fertile limestone soils and level surface made the peninsula ideal for plantation agriculture — particularly sugar cane on the broad interior plains — and dense settlement along the coast. The three comarcas of the peninsula have a combined population of approximately 280,000, with the majority concentrated in [[El Verger (comarca)|El Verger]] along the Aethiopian coast.

=== Climate ===

Migjorn has a {{w|tropical climate}} with high temperatures and abundant rainfall throughout the year. The coastal plain experiences average annual temperatures of 26–29 °C, with rainfall of 1,500–2,500 mm depending on exposure to the Aethiopian trade winds. The wet season runs from May to November, with the heaviest rainfall in the southern and eastern comarcas facing the open ocean. The dry season from December to April is shorter and less pronounced than in the Shendan-facing provinces, as the Aethiopian coast receives orographic rainfall from the trade winds year-round.

The interior hills experience slightly cooler temperatures, with average annual temperatures of 22–26 °C at elevations above 500 metres, and support crops such as cacao and coffee that require more moderate conditions. The southern comarcas, exposed to the open Aethiopian Ocean, receive the highest rainfall in the province and are subject to tropical storms during the wet season.

