The population distribution is heavily concentrated in the northern comarcas along the canal corridor and the Aethiopian coastal plain. The [[El Delta (comarca)|El Delta]] comarca, containing the provincial capital [[Sant Llàtzer]], accounts for approximately 30 per cent of the provincial population with a density of 2,507/km² — the highest of any comarca in Balboa. [[La Badia (comarca)|La Badia]], the second most populous comarca, is a large agricultural district on the Aethiopian coast with extensive sugar cane and banana plantations. Together, El Delta and La Badia account for over 53 per cent of the provincial population.

The comarcas along the [[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|E1]] and [[Expressway 4 (Balboa)|E4]] corridors — [[Les Dàrsenes (comarca)|Les Dàrsenes]], [[L'Horta del Sud (comarca)|L'Horta del Sud]], and [[L'Espigó (comarca)|L'Espigó]] — are the next most populous, with economies based on plantation agriculture, canal logistics, and port activity. The inland comarcas — [[L'Oliverar (comarca)|L'Oliverar]], [[El Vinyet (comarca)|El Vinyet]], [[La Plana d'Orient (comarca)|La Plana d'Orient]], and [[La Plana Baixa (comarca)|La Plana Baixa]] — have moderate populations supported by secondary-road connections and mixed agriculture. The remote southern comarcas — [[Cap del Sud (comarca)|Cap del Sud]], [[L'Aldea (comarca)|L'Aldea]], and [[Els Boscos (comarca)|Els Boscos]] — are the most sparsely populated, with densities below 30/km² and limited infrastructure.

== Demographics ==

With a population of approximately 5.8 million, Migjorn is the most populous province in Balboa, containing approximately 17 per cent of the national population. The population density of 189/km² is the highest of any Balboan province, reflecting the province's fertile agricultural land and the concentration of settlement along the canal corridor and the Aethiopian coast.

The ethnic composition of Migjorn reflects its plantation history and its role as a destination for Fosian immigration. [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] constitute the largest single group, descended from enslaved workers brought to the colonial sugar, banana, and cacao plantations. The Black Balboan population is concentrated in the agricultural comarcas along the coastal plain, particularly in [[La Badia (comarca)|La Badia]], [[El Verger (comarca)|El Verger]], and [[L'Horta del Sud (comarca)|L'Horta del Sud]], where plantation labour was historically concentrated.

The [[Fosian (Balboa)|Fosian]] community, primarily of Sinchewese descent, is the second largest ethnic group, concentrated in [[Sant Llàtzer]] and the canal corridor comarcas. The Fosian population is descended from workers brought to the canal and port under the [[Zong Balboa]] administration (1899–1950), when Sant Llàtzer served as the eastern terminus of the canal and a major entrepôt for interoceanic trade. The Fosian community has maintained a distinct cultural identity, with Sinchewese-derived traditions, cuisine, and religious practices that distinguish it from both the Black Balboan and White Balboan populations.

[[White Balboan people|White Balboans]] are a smaller presence in Migjorn than in the northern provinces, though communities exist in Sant Llàtzer and the administrative centres, descended from Volisanian colonial administrators and plantation owners. The proportion of White Balboans is lower than the national average, reflecting the province's plantation economy and the demographic impact of the Zong Balboa period, when much of the Volisanian planter class departed and was replaced by Fosian and Black Balboan settlers.

== Economy ==

Migjorn is the principal agricultural region of Balboa, with a plantation economy that accounts for a significant share of national agricultural output. The province produces sugar cane, bananas, rice, tropical fruits (including mango, papaya, and pineapple), and cacao, grown on large estates across the coastal plain and interior hills. [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]], the state-owned agricultural corporation, derives approximately 40 per cent of its revenue from Migjorn operations, and the province is the primary source of domestic food and export crops.

Sugar cane is the dominant crop, grown on the flat coastal plains of [[La Badia (comarca)|La Badia]] and [[El Verger (comarca)|El Verger]]. Bananas are cultivated along the Aethiopian coast, where the humid climate and reliable rainfall provide ideal conditions. Rice is grown in the low-lying areas near the canal, particularly in [[El Delta (comarca)|El Delta]] and [[L'Horta del Sud (comarca)|L'Horta del Sud]], where irrigation from the canal system supports paddy cultivation. Cacao is grown in the interior hills, where the cooler temperatures and shade provide suitable conditions, and is processed by [[Hirocorp]] under the Cacau de Migjorn brand. Tropical fruits are grown across the province for both domestic consumption and export.

[[File:Sant Llatzer canal terminus.png|thumb|right|300px|The Balboa Canal terminus at Sant Llàtzer, the principal port of Migjorn.]]
<!-- IMAGE PROMPT: Large canal lock gates at the eastern terminus of a major canal, massive concrete lock chamber with water flowing through, cargo ship transiting the lock, port facilities with cranes and warehouses in the background, tropical city skyline, palm trees along the waterfront, clear morning light, photorealistic, industrial and tropical elements blended -->

The [[Balboa Canal]] terminus at [[Sant Llàtzer]] is the principal economic asset of the province, handling interoceanic shipping and providing revenue from port fees, warehousing, and ancillary services. The canal corridor comarcas — [[El Delta (comarca)|El Delta]], [[L'Espigó (comarca)|L'Espigó]], [[La Porta (comarca)|La Porta]], and [[Les Dàrsenes (comarca)|Les Dàrsenes]] — are the most economically developed, with port facilities, logistics operations, and light manufacturing concentrated along the canal route.

[[Assegurances Heping]], a major insurance company, is headquartered in Sant Llàtzer, reflecting the city's role as a financial and commercial centre. [[Cases Justes]], the state-owned housing corporation, maintains a regional office in the city, and [[Empresa de Pastissos i Pa|EPP]], the state-owned bakery enterprise, operates a production facility in Sant Llàtzer supplying the southern provinces.

Tourism is a growing sector, centred on the Aethiopian coast beaches and the cultural attractions of Sant Llàtzer. The coastal comarcas of [[La Punta (comarca)|La Punta]] and [[El Verger (comarca)|El Verger]] have developed resort and hotel infrastructure, though the sector remains smaller than in the Sant Cristòfor area. The province's GDP is approximately $25 billion, accounting for roughly 6 per cent of national output, with a GDP per capita of approximately $4,300 — below the national average but above the poorer interior provinces.

== Transport ==

{{see also|Transport in Balboa}}

Migjorn is connected to the national transport network by four expressways. [[Expressway 1 (Balboa)|Expressway 1]], the principal north–south artery, runs through the province from the Estret border in the north to the southern comarcas, passing through Sant Llàtzer. [[Expressway 3 (Balboa)|Expressway 3]] reaches the province from the northwest, connecting the interior highlands to the coastal plain. [[Expressway 4 (Balboa)|Expressway 4]] connects [[Sant Cristòfor]] in Estret Province to Sant Llàtzer, following the canal corridor and serving as the principal freight route for canal-related commerce. [[Expressway 6 (Balboa)|Expressway 6]] runs east–west within Migjorn, connecting the inland comarcas to the canal corridor and the Aethiopian coast.

The [[Canal Line (Balboa)|Canal Line]], operated by [[Ferrocarrils de l'Istme]] (FI), is the principal railway in the province, running from Sant Cristòfor along the canal corridor to Sant Llàtzer. The line is primarily a freight corridor carrying agricultural products, canal-related cargo, and petroleum, but operates a passenger service connecting Sant Llàtzer to the national rail network. Secondary lines branch from the Canal Line to the agricultural comarcas of the coastal plain.

[[Sant Llàtzer Airport]] provides domestic flights to Sant Cristòfor, Sant Bart, and other destinations, and serves as the principal airport for the southern provinces.

== History ==

{{main|History of Balboa}}

=== Pre-colonial and colonial period ===

Prior to Volisanian colonisation, the Migjorn coastal plain was inhabited by indigenous peoples who practiced subsistence agriculture and fishing. The fertile lowlands and reliable rainfall of the Aethiopian coast supported settled communities, though the dense forest cover limited large-scale cultivation.

The Volisanian colonial administration established plantation agriculture on the Migjorn coastal plain in the 16th and 17th centuries, clearing the tropical forest for sugar cane, banana, and cacao estates. Enslaved workers were brought from Kaftia and other parts of the Volisanian colonial empire to work the plantations, and the province became the principal agricultural exporter of the Volisanian colonial system. Sant Llàtzer was developed as a port serving the plantation economy, shipping agricultural exports to Volisania and other Mesogean markets.

=== Zong Balboa period (1899–1950) ===

Following the [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899), the [[Treaty of Sant Llàtzer]] transferred the [[Balboa Canal]] and Sant Cristòfor to Zong control. The comarcas of [[L'Espigó (comarca)|L'Espigó]] and [[El Delta (comarca)|El Delta]], which straddled the canal corridor, were annexed into the [[Zong Balboa]] dependent territory. The loss of these comarcas — which included the provincial capital [[Sant Llàtzer]] — physically separated Migjorn from the northern provinces, as the Zong-controlled canal zone cut across the isthmus and severed the province's land connection to the rest of Balboa. The provincial administration was relocated to [[Campdària]] in [[La Badia (comarca)|La Badia]], which served as the de facto capital of rump Migjorn for the duration of the separation.

Under the Zong Balboa administration, Sant Llàtzer became a major entrepôt and cosmopolitan trading city. The Zong authorities encouraged Fosian immigration, particularly from the [[Sinchew]] colony, and Sinchewese workers were brought to operate the canal lock system, warehousing, and port facilities. The Zong administration introduced egalitarian laws — including universal suffrage and anti-discrimination protections — as part of a deliberate strategy to attract Black Balboan workers away from the repressive Balboan state.

The Fosian population in Migjorn grew substantially during this period, as Sinchewese, Dagit, and other Fosian settlers arrived to work in the canal zone and the surrounding agricultural comarcas. A significant portion of the Fosian diaspora chose to remain in the Balboan-administered parts of Migjorn rather than relocate to Zong Balboa territory, establishing communities in [[Campdària]], [[Sant Miquel]], and other towns along the Aethiopian coast. The Fosian community in Migjorn became proportionally larger than in any other Balboan province, shaping the province's demographic and cultural character.

=== Rump state period (1899–1973) ===

During the rump state period, Migjorn was an isolated exclave of the truncated Balboan republic, cut off from the northern provinces by the Zong-controlled canal zone. The province lost its principal economic asset — the canal and the port of Sant Llàtzer — and the agricultural economy contracted sharply, with reduced export markets and limited infrastructure investment. The southern comarcas along the Sinchew border remained remote and largely outside the effective reach of the state.

The Balboan provincial governors negotiated an arrangement with local communities under which the racial segregation laws enforced in the northern provinces were not applied in Migjorn. This compact, sustained by successive governors and local leaders, was a political exchange: in return for the non-enforcement of racial codes, the province's diverse population — including the large Fosian diaspora — remained loyal to Balboa rather than aligning with Zong Balboa. As a result, Migjorn society was notably less stratified than the northern provinces, where racial segregation and strict social hierarchies persisted throughout the rump state period.

The province's economy remained weaker than the rest of Balboa throughout the separation. Substantial economic aid was shipped from the northern provinces by coastal shipping along the Aethiopian coast, with the port of Sant Llàtzer — though under Zong Balboa administration — serving as the principal point of entry for assistance to the rest of Migjorn. The aid sustained basic services and food distribution in the provincial comarcas, but the province's infrastructure and productive capacity lagged behind the northern provinces throughout the period.

=== Post-reunification era ===

The reunification of 1973 reintegrated the canal and Sant Llàtzer into the Balboan state. Migjorn regained its position as the terminus of the Balboa Canal, and the canal corridor experienced significant infrastructure investment. The expressway network was extended to the province, with E4 connecting Sant Cristòfor to Sant Llàtzer and E6 providing east–west connectivity within the province. The agricultural sector was reorganised under the post-reunification land reform, with the largest estates restructured into cooperative and state-owned operations managed by [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]].

The post-reunification period also saw the growth of tourism along the Aethiopian coast and the development of Sant Llàtzer as a commercial and financial centre, with the establishment of [[Assegurances Heping]] and other companies in the city. The Fosian community, which had maintained its distinct identity through the rump state period, was integrated into the post-reunification Balboan state while preserving its cultural traditions.

== Culture ==

Migjorn's culture reflects the convergence of Volisanian colonial, Black Balboan, and Fosian traditions. The plantation economy produced a distinctive rural culture centred on the agricultural cycle, with harvest festivals, music, and food traditions that blend Volisanian and Kaftian elements. The Fosian community in Sant Llàtzer and the canal corridor maintains Sinchewese-derived culinary, religious, and linguistic traditions, including Baiduist practices and Sinchewese festival customs.

In sport, [[FC Migjorn Sud]], based in the provincial capital, competes in the [[LFB3]], the third tier of Balboan football. The club plays at [[Camp Palmera]] (9,500 seats) in Sant Llàtzer.

